AD  - Technische Universität Chemnitz, Germany
AB  - Binaural beats are acoustically generated frequencies that match the frequency bands of brain waves and are therefore considered a means to inducing neurophysiological relaxation. There is, however, large dispute on the scientific quality and validity of extant research on binaural beats. Thus, the effect of a binaural 10Hz (Alpha) stimulation on subjective and physiological parameters of relaxation was analysed. A total of 102 participants were randomly assigned to one of three conditions: (1) audio exposure with binaural beats, (2) audio exposure without binaural beats, (3) no sound exposure. Subjective relaxation was measured before and after the sessions. Heart rate, respiration rate, and different measures of heart rate variability were measured as physiological parameters throughout the sessions. Results did not show a specific increase in subjective or physiological relaxation due to the binaural beats treatment. The state of the art of binaural beats research is discussed in light of these findings. © 2015 Verlag Hans Huber, Hogrefe AG, Bern.
