AD  - JIS University, West Bengal, Kolkata, India
AB  - Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is a neurodevelopmental disorder characterized by attention difficulties, impulsivity, and hyperactivity. While medication and behavioral therapies are commonly used for managing ADHD symptoms, there is growing interest in exploring alternative or adjunctive interventions. Music has emerged as a potential therapeutic tool for individuals with ADHD due to its unique ability to engage multiple brain regions involved in attention, emotion regulation, and executive functions. This chapter provides an overview of the effects of music on individuals with ADHD. Research suggests that music can enhance attention and focus, reduce distractibility, and improve impulse control. It has also been found to have positive effects on emotional regulation, helping individuals with ADHD manage anxiety and mood states more effectively. Moreover, music interventions, such as music therapy or learning to play an instrument, have been associated with improvements in cognitive skills and executive functions, including working memory and cognitive flexibility. Furthermore, music can act as a stress-reduction tool, supporting individuals with ADHD in managing stress and improving overall well-being. By modulating the autonomic nervous system, music induces relaxation and tranquillity, helping alleviate the coexisting stress and anxiety often experienced by individuals with ADHD. ADHD is one of the most common neurodevelopmental disorders of childhood. It is generally first analyzed in adolescence and frequently endures into adulthood. Here, we propose an idea to predict the effect of music on ADHD patient using ML model and try to change the mood of the people by suggesting a song based on their mood. Our model will analyze the change in their overall mood. For mood detection, we use deepface, Tensorflow, CV2. Deepface uses VGG neural network. The VGG face recognition model achieves 97.78% accuracy on the popular labeled faces in the wild (LFW) dataset. For website purposes, we use frontend HTML, CSS, JAVASCRIPT, and for backend we use Django. The novelty of our work is that we have tested our model in Autism Rehabilitation Centre (Bikashayan, Denlop) and collected data from there. We also have created our own soundtrack based on the feedback from autistic children. © 2025 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
