AB  - This study is aimed to investigate the objective evaluation of mental fatigue level based on heart rate variability (HRV). First, active and passive states of fatigue were distinguished and the relationship of workload, stress and fatigue was pointed out. Mental fatigue of reading task and written calculations task was designed. Reading workload and musical relaxation was compared by HRV indexes, and written calculations task performance with HRV indexes was combined as well. Experimental results indicated that task performance had its limitation in evaluating mental fatigue degree. The fatigue accumulative effect for reading workload was stronger than that of written calculations. The increasing fatigue during reading or calculation, with increasing HRV and decreasing HR, showed the weakened capability for stress reaction. What HRV straight indicates is stress level, but with strict restriction that HRV is effective to reflect the passive mental fatigue state. HRV increases and HR decreases with increasing passive fatigue level, which might show the physiological restrainable protection.
