AD  - School of Art and Design, Wuhan Technology and Business University, China
AD  - Zhangjiawan Street Community Health Service Center, China
AB  - With increasing work pressure and sub-health conditions among modern urban populations, there is a growing need for innovative approaches to mental relaxation and stress relief. This study proposes an in-vehicle therapeutic model based on alpha wave audiovisual stimulation, integrating principles from neuroscience, environmental design, and music therapy. A multisensory experimental system was developed to explore how synchronized visual and auditory stimuli affect passengers’ psychological and physiological states. Over 50 participants were exposed to five different audiovisual scenarios, and their brain activity (EEG), heart rate variability (HRV), and self-reported mood/stress levels were monitored. The results demonstrate that alpha wave-based music, personalized visual design, and natural elements significantly enhance emotional relaxation and reduce stress. Among the five test groups, the synchronized multisensory experience showed the greatest therapeutic effectiveness. These findings suggest that alpha wave-driven audiovisual therapies offer promising potential for enhancing passenger well-being, especially in the context of autonomous and future-oriented vehicle design. © 2026 The Authors.
